; MAC-LANG-18's other half: an invocation matching NO rule names the
; macro and lists the SHAPES it accepts (2026-08-16).
;
; It used to list arities - "macro `spread` takes 1 or 3 arguments" -
; which was everything arity-selection could say and says nothing at
; all once two rules of one arity differ by shape. A reader told
; "takes 1 argument" about `(defN D)`, which passes exactly one, has
; been told the one thing that is not wrong with their program.
;
; The shapes are the rules' own patterns, spelled as the author wrote
; them: joined from the TOKENS each pattern consumed rather than
; printed back from the tree, because a float pattern holds its bits
; and a char its codepoint, and printing either back would show a
; spelling nobody wrote.
;
; Three invocations, because there are three ways to match no rule and
; the shape list is the answer to all three:
;
;   (defN D)       arity 1, which rule 1 takes - and its SHAPE does
;                  not, because `D` is not a two-element form
;   (defN D 8)     arity 2, which rule 2 takes - and `8` is not `7`,
;                  so a LITERAL is what fails
;   (defN D 8 9)   arity 3, which no rule takes at all
;
; The first two are the cases an arity list could not describe: both
; pass a count some rule declares. An earlier draft of this file
; carried only those two and claimed in this header that one of them
; matched no arity, which was false of both - caught by re-reading the
; fixture against its own header rather than by any gate.
(data D (A))

(macro defN ((defN (a b))
   (:: (syntax/join n a) Int)
   (fn ((syntax/join n a)) 1))
  ((defN T 7)
   (:: (syntax/join n T) Int)
   (fn ((syntax/join n T)) 2)))

(defN D)

(defN D 8)

(defN D 8 9)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) 0)
